@page "/privacy"
@* PRD R16: reachable from every page. Draft outline from the prototype, updated for what F04 stores; not reviewed
   legal text (D10, gate G13). *@

<SeoHead Title="Privacy · Tax Buddy" Description="What Tax Buddy keeps and who can see it. Draft pending legal review." CanonicalPath="/privacy" />

<article class="legal" aria-labelledby="page-title">
    <h1 id="page-title" tabindex="-1">Privacy</h1>
    <DraftLegalNotice />

    <h2>What Tax Buddy keeps</h2>
    <p>When you sign in with Google: your name and email address from Google, a Google identifier for your account, whether you're a client or an accountant, and when you agreed to these terms. Tax Buddy never sees your Google password. Later features will keep your profile, chats, reviews and private service work.</p>

    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>A sign-in cookie keeps you signed in on that browser for up to 14 days of inactivity, until you sign out. A security cookie protects the site's forms, and a theme cookie remembers light or dark if you choose one. None are used for advertising or tracking.</p>

    <h2>What is public</h2>
    <p>Accountant profiles, portfolio highlights, reviews and replies. A review shows the client's first name and last initial.</p>

    <h2>What is private</h2>
    <p>Client accounts are never publicly listed. Chats and service work will be visible only to the client and accountant involved. Your saved-accountant list is private to you.</p>

    <h2>Emails</h2>
    <p>Tax Buddy will email you about new messages. The emails won't include the message text, and you can turn them off.</p>

    <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
    <p>You'll be able to delete your account from your account page. What happens to your chats and reviews is still being decided (decision D07).</p>

    <p class="text-[13px] text-muted">Version @TaxBuddy.Web.Domain.LegalText.CurrentVersion.</p>
</article>
